  "gloss": "riḳhte: forms; ke: of; tumhīñ: you; ustād: master; nahīñ: not; ho: are; kahte: saying; agle: next; zamāne: time; meñ: in; koī: some; mīr: Mir (a poet's pen name); bhī: also; thā: was",
  "literal": "Forms of you are not a master Ghālib. They say in the next time some Mir also was.",
  "poetic": "Your forms are not a master, Ghālib. They claim that in the next age, even Mir was.",
  "notes": ["The wordplay here is on the multiple meanings of 'riḳhte' (forms, shapes, styles) and 'ustād' (master). Ghalib is saying that his forms, his style, is not a master, implying that he is not a great poet. The second line is a commentary on the fleeting nature of poetic fame, with 'agle zamāne' (next time) and 'koī mīr' (some Mir) being a reference to the poet Mir Taqi Mir, who was a contemporary of Ghalib's. The phrase 'bhī thā' (also was) is a Sufi idiom, implying that even the great Mir was not immune to the passage of time."],
  "lost": "The nuance of the Sufi idiom 'bhī thā' and the play on the word 'ustād' are difficult to convey in English, and the wordplay on 'riḳhte' is somewhat lost in translation."}
